# Target is set via `idf.py set-target esp32s3` (not pinned here so this file
# stays portable if a C5 build is added later per DESIGN.md §3).

# --- lwIP: required for XIAO-side IP forwarding + NAT between the HaLow
# uplink netif and the local SoftAP netif (DESIGN.md §4.2/§4.3). ---
CONFIG_LWIP_IP_FORWARD=y
CONFIG_LWIP_IPV4_NAPT=y

# (No CONFIG_LWIP_IGMP option exists in this IDF version - it's hardcoded on
# unconditionally in lwipopts.h, confirmed by a real build; nothing to set.)

# The CoT relay uses IP_PKTINFO/recvmsg() on a single dual-joined socket to
# determine which netif each multicast datagram actually arrived on, so it
# can be forwarded out the other one without risking a loop.
CONFIG_LWIP_NETBUF_RECVINFO=y

# The native 2.4GHz radio runs in exactly one mode per node, never both:
# GW_ROLE_CLIENT (default) uses it AP-only for the local SoftAP
# (downlink_softap.c), while HaLow carries that role's uplink on the separate
# SPI radio (uplink_halow.c). GW_ROLE_RELAY instead uses the native radio
# STA-only, as its own uplink to the Pi's local AP (uplink_wifi.c), while
# HaLow runs AP mode as that role's downlink (see CONFIG_HALOW_AP_MODE
# below) - see design/ROADMAP.md item 8. Neither role needs concurrent
# AP+STA on the native radio, so plain SoftAP support is sufficient here.
CONFIG_ESP_WIFI_SOFTAP_SUPPORT=y

# Real HaLow AP-mode support (genuine wpa_supplicant/hostapd code, not a
# stub - confirmed by reading esp-halow's own
# halow/components/hostap/CMakeLists.txt), needed by downlink_halow_ap.c for
# GW_ROLE_RELAY. Off by default in the component's own Kconfig because it
# "increas[es] the flash and ram requirements by a non inconsequential
# amount" (halow/Kconfig) - built into every image here anyway, since role is
# a runtime choice (gwcfg-set-role) and both roles share one firmware binary;
# a GW_ROLE_CLIENT node simply never calls the AP-mode code this pulls in.
# Marked "ALPHA NOTICE" in the SDK's own mmwlan.h and untested by this
# project on real S3+MM6108 hardware - see design/ROADMAP.md item 8 and
# design/PI_SIDE.md "GW_ROLE_RELAY" before trusting it blind on a deployed
# node.
CONFIG_HALOW_AP_MODE=y

# esp_console is used by provisioning.c for the runtime config CLI. The XIAO
# ESP32-S3's USB-C port is wired to the chip's native USB Serial/JTAG
# controller, not an external UART bridge - CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_UART_DEFAULT
# would leave both logs and the console silent over that same cable.
CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_USB_SERIAL_JTAG=y

# --- morsemicro/halow: Seeed XIAO ESP32S3 + Seeed XIAO WM6108 pin/chip/BCF
# config, copied from the component's own
# managed_components/morsemicro__halow/configs/sdkconfig.defaults.seeed_xiao_esp32s3-seeed_xiao_mm6108
# (verified against a real `idf.py build` - the component's CMake refuses to
# build at all without these). If the actual board/HAT pairing differs,
# regenerate from the matching file in that configs/ directory instead of
# hand-editing pin numbers. ---
CONFIG_MM_RESET_N=1
CONFIG_MM_WAKE=2
CONFIG_MM_BUSY=5
CONFIG_MM_SPI_SCK=7
CONFIG_MM_SPI_MOSI=9
CONFIG_MM_SPI_MISO=8
CONFIG_MM_SPI_CS=4
CONFIG_MM_SPI_IRQ=3
CONFIG_MM_BCF_FILE="bcf_fgh100mhaamd.bin"
CONFIG_MM_CHIP_MM6108=y

# Power-save OFF, because this HAT does not wire the two pins it needs.
#
# This is NOT in the vendor's board file and is NOT cleanup - do not drop it.
# The component's own Kconfig (halow/Kconfig L20-27) declares:
#
#     config HALOW_PS_MODE
#         bool "Enable Morse Micro HaLow Powersave Mode"
#         default true
#         depends on MM_WAKE >= 0
#         depends on MM_BUSY >= 0
#         help
#             This can only be set to true for devices which have the BUSY and
#             WAKE pins connected.
#
# Both dependencies are satisfied by the CONFIG_MM_WAKE=2 / CONFIG_MM_BUSY=5
# above, so without this line the symbol takes its `default true` and
# power-save runs. But on the Seeed HAT (WI-FI_HALOW_FGH100M_EXT01 rev V3.0)
# those two links are the board's only DNP resistors - R10 (D1->WAKEUP_IN) and
# R17 (D4->BUSY) are unpopulated, with WAKEUP_IN pulled high through R9 and
# BUSY pulled to GND through R15 (see design/HARDWARE.md). The precondition in
# that help text is therefore not met: the driver drives and samples pins that
# reach nothing.
#
# What that costs at runtime, reading the driver:
#   - morse_ps_sleep() (morselib src/driver/morse_driver/ps.c) deasserts WAKE
#     and calls morse_trns_set_irq_enabled(false). The module ignores the WAKE
#     line - R9 holds WAKEUP_IN high, so it never actually sleeps - and keeps
#     running while the host has stopped servicing its SPI interrupt.
#   - morse_ps_wakeup() -> morse_ps_wait_after_wake_pin_raise() then blocks on
#     a semaphore given only by the BUSY edge ISR, which cannot fire on a pin
#     tied to GND, so every wake burns the full chip wakeup delay.
#
# Bus errors accumulated that way reach comms_op_check() (transport/sdio.c
# L120-137) -> mmdrv_host_health_check_required() -> a failed health check ->
# mmdrv_host_hw_restart_required(), whose handler ends in
# MMOSAL_ASSERT(mmdrv_init(...) == MMWLAN_SUCCESS) (umac/umac_mmdrv_shim.c
# L89). A failed assert is not a returned error here: mmosal_impl_assert()
# calls mmhal_reset() -> esp_restart() (shims/mmhal_os.c L45-51), so the whole
# node reboots. Symptom is a boot loop that starts a second or two after
# "Attempting to connect to: <ssid>" and never reaches the connect timeout.
#
# With this off, the shim compiles out the WAKE/BUSY GPIO setup and the BUSY
# ISR entirely (shims/mmhal_wlan.c L43-73, L288-326) and mmhalow_init() calls
# mmwlan_set_power_save_mode(MMWLAN_PS_DISABLED) (mmhalow.c L199-201).
# The cost is real - no radio power save, which matters for a battery build -
# and the fix if that becomes the binding constraint is populating R10/R17 on
# the HAT, not flipping this back.
#
# Upstream does offer a second way out. shims/CMakeLists.txt L36-44 rejects a
# build that sets exactly one of the two pins, with: "MM_WAKE and MM_BUSY pins
# need to either both be defined and routed for power saving, or both need to
# be disabled (-1 defaut definition) to mark this as intentional and forgoe
# power saving" [sic]. So CONFIG_MM_WAKE=-1 + CONFIG_MM_BUSY=-1 would also
# work, and would make power-save structurally unselectable rather than merely
# off. It is deliberately not what's done here: it edits the pin numbers copied
# verbatim from upstream's board config, which is the one part of this file
# design/HARDWARE.md tells you never to hand-edit, and it would imply D1/D4 are
# free pads when a later HAT revision could populate R10/R17. Disabling the
# feature while leaving the pin map alone keeps both documents honest.
CONFIG_HALOW_PS_MODE=n

# HaLow needs a regulatory/country code to know its legal channel list
# (DESIGN.md §6.5). This is a *build-time* setting - unlike uplink
# SSID/security it cannot be changed later via gwcfg-* at runtime, because
# mmhalow_init() reads it directly from Kconfig.
#
# "US" is not a fallback or a placeholder here - it is the only value this
# hardware supports, and this line is now the single source of truth for it
# (CI builds it unmodified; there is no country-configs/ layering any more).
#
# The module is a Quectel FGH100M-H, a 902-928 MHz part. The BCF pinned above,
# CONFIG_MM_BCF_FILE="bcf_fgh100mhaamd.bin", is named "FGH100M-H (US)" in Morse
# Micro's own morse-firmware/WHENCE.md (L53) - and that BCF carries a real
# per-regdom calibration payload for US only. Setting a different code here does
# not fail the build; it produces firmware whose radio never associates. See
# design/HARDWARE.md "Regulatory domain" for the section-by-section evidence.
#
# Still a *build-time* setting - unlike uplink SSID/security it cannot be
# changed later via gwcfg-* at runtime, because mmhalow_init() reads it directly
# from Kconfig (halow/mmhalow.c L191).
CONFIG_HALOW_COUNTRY_CODE="US"

# --- Partition table: the default single-app table only allows a 1MB app
# partition, but this firmware (HaLow SDK + softAP + NAT + CoT relay +
# console + web UI) doesn't fit - a real `idf.py build` failed with "app
# partition is too small ... overflow 0x93970" (~600KB over) against the
# default, and still didn't fit the built-in "large" preset (1500K). Custom
# 3MB app partition, confirmed real flash size is 8MB (this board's actual
# spec, not a conservative guess) so there's plenty of margin for growth
# (self-beacon, bigger web UI, etc.) without revisiting this again soon. ---
CONFIG_PARTITION_TABLE_CUSTOM=y
CONFIG_PARTITION_TABLE_FILENAME="partitions.csv"
CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHSIZE_8MB=y

# --- Core dumps to flash (partitions.csv has a 64K coredump partition).
# Hardware bring-up means crashes will happen while nobody is attached to the
# console; without this a panic leaves nothing behind but a lost session. With
# it, `idf.py coredump-info` reconstructs the backtrace and task states after
# the fact. ELF format because it carries task/stack detail the raw binary
# format doesn't. ---
CONFIG_ESP_COREDUMP_ENABLE_TO_FLASH=y
CONFIG_ESP_COREDUMP_DATA_FORMAT_ELF=y
CONFIG_ESP_COREDUMP_CHECKSUM_CRC32=y
